{"id":4405,"date":"2021-12-06T15:22:01","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:22:01","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4405"},"modified":"2024-12-02T21:59:28","modified_gmt":"2024-12-03T05:59:28","slug":"2-6-timers-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-6-timers-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"2.6: Timers"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b09dfd2\" name=\"6a2213b09dfd2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b09e102\" name=\"6a2213b09e102\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Succes<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain how computers measure time<\/li>\n<li>Describe what an epoch time is and how it impacts time calculations<\/li>\n<li>Describe the calculations that occur when we add or subtract time<\/li>\n<li>Store and use time values in variables and lists in a program<\/li>\n<li>Use variables to store times and report the duration of flight movements and programs<\/li>\n<li>Limit program time using timers<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>How Does the Timer Work?<\/li>\n<li>Total Flight Timer<\/li>\n<li>Movement Timer<\/li>\n<li>Using Timers in a Function<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: One Minute Code<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: LED Flight Time Warning<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b09e170\" name=\"6a2213b09e170\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>How Does the Timer Work?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Timers are everywhere so they might not seem like a big deal, but being able to use a timer in your program is an incredibly useful and powerful skill! This can help you keep better track of your flights and the movements within them.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>You may remember back when we first learned how to import and create our drone that we discussed\u00a0<strong>objects.\u00a0<\/strong>We also mentioned that objects have\u00a0<strong>properties\u00a0<\/strong>and\u00a0<strong>methods.\u00a0<\/strong>Much like our drone object we create in each program is a virtual representation of our physical drone, we can create a timer object that keeps track of time much like a timer on your phone or watch would.<\/p>\n<p>We have already used the time library before when we made our drone <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">time.sleep(seconds)<\/code>. Now we will use it to check and the time at various points in our code and perform some simple math. Before we do this, let&#8217;s take a look at the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">time.time()<\/code> function and see how it works and what value it gets:<\/p>\n<p>https:\/\/youtu.be\/4umPRQv022U[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b09e1f7\" name=\"6a2213b09e1f7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Total Flight Timer<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For this activity, you will be programming a timer to keep track of your CoDrone EDU\u2019s total flight, starting at takeoff and finishing with landing.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the time libraries, create a variable to store the drone\u2019s starting time (we called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_time<\/code>)<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. Have your drone take off and land, and then create another variable to store the drone\u2019s ending time (we called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">end_time<\/code>)<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To get the total time of your drone\u2019s flight, create a variable that will subtract <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_time<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> from <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">end_time<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. We called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">elapsed_time<\/code>.<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> Finally, use a <strong>print<\/strong> command to have your program print the elapsed time in seconds.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Once you have tried this out, add hovering and then some flight movements into your program. <em>What changes do you notice?<\/em><\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your code should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\nimport time\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nstart_time = time.time()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\nend_time = time.time()\r\n\r\n\r\nelapsed_time = end_time - start_time\r\n\r\nprint(elapsed_time)\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b09e283\" name=\"6a2213b09e283\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Movement Timer<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>What if you wanted to check how long one section in your program took, like how long your drone takes to fly in one square, or if pitching forward for 2 seconds actually takes 2 seconds?<\/em> There\u2019s a way to do that, and it just involves moving some timers around in your program!<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For this exercise, we will pretend that we are programming a movie videography drone. The director wants a 360 degree continuous shot around the two actors for an epic final scene! They need at least 30 seconds of video to cut into the movie. For this program, we will create a loop that lasts 30 seconds using a time and fly in a circle around the actors.<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the \u201cSequence\u201d and time libraries, have your drone take off.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Next, we need to create the variables for the loop. We will create a <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_time<\/code> that will capture the time right before the loop. We will also create an <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">end_time<\/code> and assign it the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_time<\/code> variable value + 30 seconds for the required flight time:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nstart_time = time.time()\r\nend_time= start_time + 30\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Now, we need to create the loop. To do this, we will compare the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_time<\/code>to the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">end_time<\/code> and as long as it is less than the end time, we keep running the circle.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.set_pitch(50)\r\ndrone.set_yaw(50)\r\nwhile start_time &lt; end_time:\r\n    drone.move()\r\n    start_time = time.time() #update the time once the sequence is done\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>We have to update the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_time<\/code> after each sequence, or it would end up creating an infinite loop. <em>Can you figure out why?<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Now, have your drone land. To check if our flight time was enough, add a line that prints out the difference between start time and end time.<em> Did we fly long enough? Was it exactly 30 seconds?<\/em> If the time wasn&#8217;t exact, <em>why do you think this is the case?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b09e2fe\" name=\"6a2213b09e2fe\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using Timers in a Function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Creating multiple variables to time one thing can be a lot. In cases when you want to time one movement, you can create a function instead! This will run a timer for the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">move()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function for however many seconds you put in the parameters.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the time libraries, define a function (we called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">fly()<\/code>)<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> and put <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">seconds<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> in the parameters. Next, create a variable to store the drone\u2019s starting time. Create a while loop so that if the time minus the start time is less than the seconds in the parameters, the drone will move.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, have your drone take off and set the pitch to 30. Include the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">fly()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0function and set the parameters to 2 seconds, and then have the drone land.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Play around with the time in the parameters. <em>What happens? Does increasing or decreasing the pitch have an impact on the time?<\/em><\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your code should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\nimport time\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n# -------- function definition ---------\r\n\r\n\r\ndef fly(seconds):\r\n\r\n    start_time = time.time()\r\n\r\n    while time.time() - start_time &lt; seconds:\r\n\r\n        drone.move()\r\n\r\n# --------------------------------------\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_pitch(30)\r\nfly(2)\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b09e36c\" name=\"6a2213b09e36c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 1: One Minute Code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fit as much flight movement as you can into one minute exactly (according to the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">elapsed_time<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">variable in your program)!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rules:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You must include at least one pitch, one throttle, one yaw, and one roll.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Any flight movement, including hovering or delaying, can only last for 3 seconds.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The same movement cannot be used in consecutive order, whether that is one after the other or in a loop (so don\u2019t loop pitching forward for 3 seconds 20 times).<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Takeoff and landing are included in the one minute.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b09e3d0\" name=\"6a2213b09e3d0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 2: Alarm Clock<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, create a program that takes a user input and sets a timer to wait for an amount of time equal to that input. After the timer has finished, have your drone take off and act as an alarm clock, changing LED colors and playing sounds. In order to complete this challenge, your program must have the following:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A user input prompt asking for the amount of time for the timer in milliseconds<\/li>\n<li>The input must be converted into an integer to be used for the timer<\/li>\n<li>You must have at least two different LED colors and three different sounds after the timer has finished<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b09e419\" name=\"6a2213b09e419\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about the time Python library! You learned how your computer measures time, what the epoch is, and how to use the time library to calculate the elapsed time in a program.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 10 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-6-timers-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4732,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,4328,14401,2122,2134,2139],"class_list":["post-4405","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-timers","module-intermediate","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4405","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":25,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4405\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13324,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4405\/revisions\/13324"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4732"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4405"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4405"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4405"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}